/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Concrete is cement, sand, coarse aggregate and water hardened into a stony mass.
   - Strength is measured by crushing 150 mm cubes after 28 days of curing.
   - IS 456 sets a minimum grade for reinforced concrete by exposure: M20 for mild exposure, M25 for moderate, M30 for severe, M35 for very severe and M40 for extreme.
   - Two more properties come from fck.
   - The real compressive curve climbs, bends over, peaks near a strain of 0.002 and then falls as the concrete crushes.
   - Bars are graded by their characteristic yield strength fy in N/mm².
   - All grades have the same modulus, Es = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm², about eight times the modulus of M25 concrete.
   - IS 456 gives two design curves, both with a design yield stress fyd = fy/1.15 = 0.87fy.
   - SP 16, the design aid to IS 456, tabulates this curve: for Fe415 it runs from 288.7 N/mm² at a strain of 0.00144 to 360.9 N/mm² at 0.00380.
   - Creep is the slow growth of strain under a load that stays on.
*/
